        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> * It no longer respects my `.gitignore` (e.g. shows results inside `node_modules` directory)\r\n> \r\n> * My temporary rg config has a glob which just ignores anything inside the `.git` directory, it's not respecting that as well\r\n\r\nYeah, so this is the effect of using extra `--glob`, not that it doesn't respect config file. Based on the `man rg`, together with that config search was essentially the `rg --glob=!.git/ --glob=*` (in this specific order of `--glob`, because arguments from config file are put first in the command). This has the effect that `--glob=*` \"overrides\" the previous glob.\r\n\r\nThe fact that `.gitignore` is not respected when there is at least one `--glob` argument seems to be by design (BurntSushi/ripgrep#2252). Not sure why this doesn't conflict with `--glob=!git/` in the config file, but that is to the best.\r\n\r\n------\r\n\r\nI've force pushed the updated version to [`pick-glob`](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/tree/pick-glob) branch. Now it:\r\n- Allows array `globs` instead of a single string `glob`. Pressing `<C-o>` will *add* glob to the array allowing things like `{ '*.ts', '!node_modules/', '!.git/' }` to filter files.\r\n- Explicitly does nothing if there is no `glob` supplied. This should at least preserve the gitignore behavior when no manual glob patterns are supplied.\r\n\r\nWould you mind giving it a second spin, please?",
