        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "Interestingly, now I no longer get that issue of showing me gitignored files, and now `!.git` is respected — So everything is working correctly on my end!\r\n\r\n> The fact that .gitignore is not respected when there is at least one --glob argument seems to be by design (https://github.com/BurntSushi/ripgrep/issues/2252). Not sure why this doesn't conflict with --glob=!git/ in the config file, but that is to the best.\r\n\r\nI'm curious why `.gitignore` is still respected in my case, in any case that's what I intend so that's fine by me 🤷🏻‍♂️\r\n\r\nEDIT: Upon reading ripgrep's author explanation carefully it does seem that it overrides by taking precedence over whatever I have in gitignore. In that issue the user was using `**` as the glob, so then `.gitignore` is ignored since that pattern allows everything that is being ignored by the gitignore. In my case I'm simply passing `!.git` as the glob which doesn't overlap at all with my `.gitignore` contents, thus gitignore is respected.",
